course: arts
lesson: 9
title: Instrument families: strings, wind, brass, percussion, keys
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Instruments are sorted into families by HOW they make their sound, not by what they are made of: strings shake strings, woodwinds use breath across an edge or on a reed, brass players buzz their lips, and percussion is hit, shaken or scraped.
explain: That is why the shiny metal saxophone is a woodwind (it uses a reed, a thin vibrating strip of cane) and the metal flute is a woodwind too (you blow across an edge), while the trumpet is brass because your lips must buzz.
explain: Picture a family photo of strings: violin, viola, cello and double bass, from small to huge; in every family the bigger ones play lower notes, like a tiny dog yapping and a big dog woofing.
explain: Some instruments do not fit one box: a piano has strings struck by hammers when you press keys, so people argue whether it is strings or percussion, and many just call it a keyboard instrument.
explain: Around the world people talk about it their own way: in New England "wicked" means very, in New Zealand "chur" means thanks or cheers, and an "amp" is an amplifier that makes an electric guitar loud.
fact: A piano's strings are hit by small felt hammers when you press the keys, so people argue about its family and many call it a keyboard instrument.
fact: The saxophone is made of brass but is a woodwind, because it makes its sound with a reed, like a clarinet.
fact: Flutes used to be made of wood and some still are; the sound comes from blowing across an edge, so the flute is a woodwind.
fact: Brass players buzz their lips into the mouthpiece; blowing without buzzing gives just a hiss.
fact: The violin, viola, cello and double bass are all string instruments played with a bow or plucked; bigger ones play lower notes, and the cello and double bass are so big that they stand on the floor instead of resting under the chin like a violin.
fact: Percussion means instruments that are hit, shaken or scraped; some, like the xylophone and the timpani (big orchestra drums tuned to notes), play real notes.
fact: The tuba is the biggest and lowest brass instrument, and the trumpet is small and high; the player's size does not matter.
fact: Orchestras usually seat the quieter strings at the front and the louder brass and percussion at the back, to balance the sound.
fact: An electric guitar has a solid body and is quiet until it is plugged into an amplifier; an acoustic guitar has a hollow body that makes it louder without electricity.
fact: The ukulele grew out of small Portuguese guitars brought to Hawaii by immigrants from Madeira in the 1870s and 1880s, and was developed in Hawaii.
fact: Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow so the hair grips the strings and makes them vibrate; traditional bow hair is horsehair.
fact: The family of an instrument depends on how the sound is made, not on its material.
exercise: Ahmed says strings, Lucy says percussion. What is true about the piano? | Its strings are hit by hammers, so people argue | It is clearly a string instrument with no debate | It is clearly a percussion instrument with no debate | It is a brass instrument, because of its shiny pedals
exercise: Erin says the saxophone is brass because it is made of brass. Why is she wrong? | It makes sound with a reed, so it is a woodwind | It is made of wood, not brass at all | It is a string instrument hidden inside a curly brass tube | Families are decided by colour, not by sound
exercise: Kaito asks why the metal flute is a woodwind. What is the reason? | You blow across an edge instead of buzzing your lips | All metal instruments are secretly made of wood on the inside | The flute is played with a wooden stick | It was put there by mistake long ago
exercise: Ruby blows into a trumpet and only gets air. What is missing? | Buzzing her lips into the mouthpiece | Blowing much harder and faster into the bell | Pressing all three valves down at once | Holding the trumpet upside down to play
exercise: Sasha says a cello is "just a big violin". What is true? | Same family, but different size, range, sound and way of holding | Exactly the same instrument as a violin, only much bigger and slower | A cello is a brass instrument | A cello is played with the mouth
exercise: In the string family, which plays the lowest notes? | The double bass | The violin, the smallest | The viola | The ukulele
exercise: Isha says the xylophone can't be percussion because it plays a tune. What is true? | Many percussion instruments play tunes | Percussion can never play notes | The xylophone is really a string instrument | Only drums are real percussion
exercise: The big fella plays tuba, little Ella plays trumpet. What decides the sound? | The instrument's size, not the player's | The size of the person who is playing it | The age of the person playing it | How loud the audience claps
exercise: Why are violins at the front and drums at the back? | To balance the quieter and louder sounds | The best players always get to sit at the front | The drummers asked to hide away at the back | The violins arrived first and grabbed the seats
exercise: Finn's electric guitar is quiet with nothing plugged in. What is going on? | It needs an amplifier to be loud | It is broken and needs throwing away | Electric guitars are always the loudest | He needs to strum much harder and faster
exercise: Keanu says the ukulele was invented in Hawaii, 100 percent. What is fairer? | It grew from Portuguese guitars brought by Madeiran immigrants | It was invented in Portugal and never reached Hawaii at all | It has nothing to do with Hawaii at all | It was invented in a factory in Ohio
exercise: Nina rubs a sticky block on her bow. What is it? | Rosin, so the bow hair grips the strings | Crayon, to colour in the strings so they shine | Soap, to keep the bow clean | Glue, to hold the bow together
exercise: What is traditional bow hair made from? | Horsehair | Human hair | Nylon fishing line | Cotton thread
exercise: What decides an instrument's family? | How its sound is made | What material it is made of | How big and heavy the instrument is | Which country it came from
exercise: What are timpani? | Big tuned drums in an orchestra | Small Hawaiian guitars | A kind of reed | The keys on a piano
test: Your mate goes, "anything shiny and metal is brass, innit." Which instrument proves him wrong? | The flute, which is metal but a woodwind | The trumpet, which is brass | The tuba, which is brass | The trombone, which is brass and slides in and out
test: What would happen if a violinist forgot to put rosin on the bow? | The bow would slide and hardly make the string sound | The violin would get much louder and brighter than before | The strings would snap at once | Nothing at all would change
test: A cartoon shows a trumpeter blowing like he's cooling soup and a fanfare comes out. What's wrong? | Brass needs buzzing lips, not just air | Nothing, that is exactly how trumpets work | Trumpets play only when shaken | Trumpets need rosin to play
test: Finn in Auckland says, "Chur, but it's broken, bro." ("chur" means cheers) What should he check first? | Whether the guitar is plugged into an amp | Whether the strings are made of gold | Whether he is holding it upside down | Whether it has enough rosin
test: A dad in Boston says the big lad on tuba is "wicked funny" (very funny). What is the truth about tuba and size? | Anyone can play a tuba, and it plays low because it is big | Only big people are allowed to play a tuba in a band | Tubas make high notes because they're big | Tubas shrink to fit the player
test: Which question tells you an instrument's family? | How does it make its sound? | What colour is it? | How much did it cost? | Who played it first?
test: An orchestra puts the drums right at the front. What would most likely happen? | The drums would drown out the quieter strings | The concert would sound more balanced and exciting for everyone | Nobody would hear the drums | The strings would get louder
test: Your cousin says the piano is "obviously strings, end of". What is the fairest answer? | It has strings, but hammers hit them, so people argue | He's right, nobody anywhere has ever argued about it | It is a wind instrument | It is a brass instrument
test: Isha's brother reckons percussion "can't do tunes". Which instrument proves him wrong? | The xylophone | The snare drum | The tambourine | The maracas
test: Nina asks why we put "crayon" on the bow. What is the real answer? | It is rosin from pine trees, to make the hair grip | It is crayon, to colour the strings | It is wax, to make the bow slippery | It is chalk, to dry the strings
test: Which plays highest: violin, viola, cello or double bass? | Violin | Viola | Cello | Double bass
exam: Identify the principal criterion upon which instruments are assigned to families. | The means by which the sound is produced | The material of which the instrument is constructed | The dimensions of the instrument | The nationality of its inventor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of the saxophone. | It is a woodwind instrument, since its sound is produced by a reed | It is a brass instrument, since it is constructed of brass | It is a string instrument concealed within a tube | It is a percussion instrument, since it is struck
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an electric guitar produces little sound when not connected to any apparatus. | Its solid body requires an amplifier to render its sound loud | It has sustained irreparable damage to its body and strings and cannot be mended | It is designed to be inaudible in all circumstances | Its strings require the application of rosin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the customary seating of an orchestra? | Quieter strings are placed at the front and louder brass and percussion at the rear | The most accomplished players are placed at the front | The percussion is placed nearest the audience | Seating is determined by the order of arrival
